From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 13:00:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] indexeddb: Create transactions without waiting for the
 backend

IDBDatabase.transaction() sent CreateTransaction and blocked the script
thread until the backend replied with the transaction's id, a number
from one counter in the manager. Gecko, Chromium and WebKit create
transactions without a synchronous message.

Transactions are now named by IndexedDBTxnId: the id of their
connection and a serial number that is unique among the connection's
transactions. Script numbers the transactions of a connection from 1
and sends CreateTransaction with the id, without a reply; the backend
gives the upgrade transaction of an open request serial 0, with the
request's id, which is also the connection's. Every message about a
transaction carries the id (commit, abort, requests, schema operations,
RequestHandled, TransactionFinished, and the replies), so P2.2's
results per connection can be tagged with it. The manager's global
counter and the unused UpgradeVersion message are gone.

Transaction scheduling ("created before") follows the order in which
CreateTransaction messages reach the manager, as Chromium orders them.
Script sends the message before transaction() returns, ahead of any
other message about the transaction, and every global's messages reach
the manager through its one channel. So two transactions whose
creation is ordered in one global, also on two connections, or across
globals through anything that orders them (a postMessage, a lock),
are created in that order in the backend, as with the synchronous
message before.

A CreateTransaction for a database that is gone is ignored, and
requests for a database that is gone now fail with DbNotFound instead
of being dropped; before, script panicked when CreateTransaction
failed.

Validation: host `cargo nextest run -p servo-storage -p servo-storage-traits`: 105 passed, 1 skipped, as before (the tests use the new ids; the integration tests create transactions without waiting, like script). Host SpiderMonkey WPT IndexedDB/ plus /_mozilla/IndexedDB/ (8 processes): 3,251 of 4,511 IndexedDB subtests pass, as at P1's tip, and all /_mozilla/ ones; no unexpected result. The transaction-scheduling-*.any.js tests and idbtransaction-oncomplete.any.js, all globals, `--repeat 10 --processes 4`: 170 of 170 OK (plus the expected serviceworker ERRORs).
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; the agent wrote the change, adapted the tests, and ran the host tests and WPT)
Signed-off-by: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
